//! Collector CSV export and import.
//!
//! One row per printing, with enough columns that a person can read it in a spreadsheet and the
//! app can import it back without losing which printing it was. Every value comes from the card
//! database and the product catalog (`hocg_products.json`), both regenerated by the assets repo,
//! or the model's URL helpers, so new products, sets and promos need no change here. The catalog
//! is optional: without it, the set names and `Release Date` are empty. Lists are one value per line (a quoted cell with line breaks), or joined with ` / ` when
//! multi-line cells are off. The export has a release (the printings: ids, URLs, images, dates)
//! and a name language (card, set, card type and color names), so English names can go with
//! Japanese cards. The columns are, in order (`Deck` is only written for decks):
//!
//! | Column         | Content                                                                    |
//! |----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `Deck`         | `Oshi`, `Main Deck` or `Cheer Deck` (decks only; its presence makes import rebuild a deck) |
//! | `Quantity`     | Number of copies                                                           |
//! | `Card Number`  | e.g. `hBP09-094`                                                           |
//! | `Name`         | Name in the name language, or the other language when missing             |
//! | `Rarity`       | Rarity code, e.g. `U`, `P`, `OSR`                                          |
//! | `Set Code`     | Expansion codes of the products the printing is in; without products, the image's set folder (see [`image_set_code`]) |
//! | `Set Name`     | Short names, in the name language, else their unofficial translation, else the other language's |
//! | `Full Set Name` | Catalog names, in the name language (else the other one), of the products the printing is in, in the release (`products`, else the other language's); codes missing from the catalog are left out |
//! | `Release Date` | Earliest catalog release date in the release among the printing's products in it (`YYYY-MM-DD`) |
//! | `Illustrator`  | As credited (mostly Japanese), empty when none                             |
//! | `Language`     | `JP` or `EN`: the release, or the other one for a printing released only in it (the row's ids, URLs and images are from it) |
//! | `Card ID`      | Official card ids in the release (Deck Log `manage_id`), the import key  |
//! | `Variant`      | Image path without extension (release, else the other one), e.g. `hPR/hBP09-094_P_02`; identifies printings without a card id |
//! | `Official URL` | Official card pages in the release, else the other language's             |
//! | `Yuyu-tei URL` | Yuyu-tei product pages                                                     |
//! | `TCGplayer URL`| TCGplayer product pages                                                    |
//! | `Image URL`    | Card image                                                                 |
//! | `Proxy URL`    | Proxy image in the release, for a printing not released in it (the row is the other release) |
//! | `Notes`        | e.g. `Not released in English (proxy)`                                    |
//!
//! Import is header driven (case-insensitive, with aliases), accepts comma, semicolon or tab
//! delimiters and a UTF-8 BOM. Each row is matched, in order, by: Card ID (+ Language) or
//! Official URL, Variant or Image URL, TCGplayer URL or id, Yuyu-tei URL, then card number narrowed by
//! rarity, set code, set and illustrator, falling back to the basic printing. Cells may hold
//! several values, one per line or separated by spaces, `/`, `,`, `;` or `|`.

